// The network's sizes (sockets.mlx and tcp.mlx import each other: the
// numbers they share live here).
// The sockets the machine has. The table is static, a socket's place in
// it some 100 bytes and a connection's 350 (tcp.mlx), so 128 of them are
// 56 KiB of the kernel's own memory whether they are used or not; the
// rings (below) are only taken when a socket opens, 32 KiB a connection:
// 4 MiB with every one of them a connection. What is looked through for
// a segment that comes, and on every tick of the timer, is the table:
// 128 places are a few hundred nanoseconds. (It was 32, which a program
// with a connection a page part and a listener beside it used up, and
// which 31 clients that never closed their side took whole.)
pub const MAX_SOCKETS: usize = 128
// A socket's receive ring, a connection's send ring (bytes).
pub const RING: usize = 16384
